import Native

{-
   `String.slice` on JavaScript under the legacy resolve stage - a copy, and the signature says so.

   `String.yana` has the contract; `String.ownership.js.yana` beside this is the window signature the
   rewrite reads, over the same host call. This body was in `String.yana` until 2026-09-21 and moved
   out for the reason the native pair moved out before it: the two stages differ in the *signature*,
   so choosing between them after reading both is not an option. Goes with `compiler/resolve`.

   `self.substring(from, to)` is the engine's own copy. The unit loop it replaces was a concatenation
   per unit, so cutting one line out of a text walked the rope once per unit of the line and
   splitting a file was quadratic in the size of the file. The bounds are clamped here rather than
   left to the host's own clamping, because the native half has to clamp and the two have to answer
   the same thing.
-}
pub fn String.slice(text: String, from: Size, to: Size) -> String:
    let count = length(text)
    let &start = from
    if start < 0 then start = 0

    let &end = to
    if end > count then end = count
    if start >= end then return ""

    return hostSubstring(text, start, end)

{-
   Duplicating one, on the stage whose JS `String` is a compiler primitive - `String.yana` is where
   the argument is.

   The body is the value: a host string is immutable and has no storage to share, so two names for
   one of them cannot observe each other. Under the rewrite the record is `TrivialCopy` and the
   blanket instance says the same thing, so this is the legacy stage's alone.
-}
instance Copy(String):
    fn copy(from: String) -> String = from
